WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.860
In this age of CGI, it's easy for filmmakers
to use digital effects instead of actual stunts.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.120
And while the end result might look cool,
it doesn't quite pack the same punch.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.000
However, some of the most incredible movie
scenes involve practical stunts that are so

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.400
unbelievable you’d never guess they were
totally real.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.720
Terminator 2: Judgment Day

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.640
When it comes to pushing the envelope in special
effects, James Cameron is without a doubt

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:25.540
the king of the world.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:31.120
But this VFX revolutionary still believes
in using practical effects whenever possible.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.020
After all, we’re talking about the guy who
built an 800-foot Titanic replica and then

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.900
sank it in a 5 million-gallon water tank.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:43.080
However, in Terminator 2, Cameron took his
love for real-life stunts to death-defying

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:43.600
levels.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.040
Near the end of the film, the 3-time Oscar
winner used an actual aircraft during a thrilling

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.400
chase where a SWAT van attempts to outrun
a helicopter.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:56.000
In one stunt, the chopper flies beneath an
overpass at nearly 70 miles per hour.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.700
But with just five feet of room above the
helicopter, the camera crew refused to film

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.220
it.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:04.560
So Cameron shot the sequence himself, pulling
off what's got to be one of the riskiest aircraft

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.960
stunts in cinema history.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:08.320
Speed

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:14.499
Before he was Neo or John Wick, Keanu Reeves
was Jack Traven, a policeman who finds himself

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.340
aboard a bus that's rigged to explode if it
drops below 50 miles per hour.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.660
Things get even worse when the bus races up
an elevated freeway, straight toward a 50-foot

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:24.540
gap in the road.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.200
Sandra Bullock’s character, Annie, is forced
to drive the bus straight into the air to

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.060
clear the gap, landing safely on the other
side.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.880
The filmmakers digitally removed that missing
chunk of the highway, but director Jan de

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.420
Bont actually got the bus to fly.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.810
Using a vehicle that was stripped down to
bare bones, their stunt driver got the bus

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:46.200
up to 61 miles per hour before soaring off
a ramp built on an LA freeway.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.640
"Thank you, thank you very much."

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.460
Mission: Impossible II

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.289
When it comes to performing his own stunts,
Tom Cruise is miles above nearly every other

00:01:55.289 --> 00:01:56.320
actor on the planet.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.200
For proof, look no further than Mission: Impossible
2.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.180
In the opening sequence, Cruise dangles off
the side of a cliff without a safety net.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.500
So when he flings himself from one rock to
another, the only thing keeping him alive

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:08.380
is a thin cable.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:13.310
Later on, during an insane fight sequence,
Cruise had a steel cable attached to a knife

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:16.740
and positioned himself so it would stop about
a quarter of an inch away from his eye.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.860
It's that kind of dedication that proves Tom
Cruise is worth every cent he makes…and

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.580
also that he's totally nuts.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.740
The Bourne Ultimatum

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.140
In the third installment of the Bourne series,
secret agent Jason Bourne throws down with

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.220
an assassin in what may be the best fight
of the franchise.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.180
But first, Bourne has to leap across an alleyway
and crash through a window.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.840
Rest assured, there's no CGI here, just old-fashioned
practical effects.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:48.450
Matt Damon’s body double leapt from one
building to another, smashing through a false

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:52.520
window, while another stunt man, attached
to a wire rig, followed with a handheld camera.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:54.020
Skyfall

00:02:54.760 --> 00:03:00.600
The 007 franchise is jam-packed with amazing
stunts, from the parkour sequence in Casino

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.400
Royale to the parachute jump in The Spy Who
Loved Me.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.050
But it's pretty hard to beat the motorcycle
chase from Skyfall.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:11.170
Instead of using a green screen, director
Sam Mendes decided to put actual motorbikes

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.260
on actual rooftops in the Middle East.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.640
The stunt drivers maneuvered their bikes down
concrete strips that were about a meter wide.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.540
Bond stunt double Robbie Maddison explained,

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.400
"A lot of the rooftops have old air conditioning
units with metal stakes coming up.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.300
Things that can stab you if you were to fall
on them.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.120
It definitely was not a place you'd want to
ride with no helmet."

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.020
Iron Man 3

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:35.380
We can all agree that one of the best action
sequences in the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:40.260
is Iron Man 3's "Barrel of Monkeys" scene,
when everyone aboard Air Force One is sucked

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:40.820
out of the plane.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.730
The freefalling crewmembers form a human chain,
allowing Iron Man to safely deposit them into

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:46.240
the ocean.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:50.680
Marvel's executives gave their movie wings
— by hiring the Red Bull stunt team for

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:51.480
this sequence.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:55.440
In total, these diehard adrenaline junkies
made approximately 600 jumps over the course

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.000
of eight days, with each dive lasting about
a minute.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:58.680
While the VFX crew had to take care of adding
certain aspects — like Air Force One, the

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:00.040
"Excellent.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.340
Good team effort, all around.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:02.320
Go us."

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:04.840
The Fast and The Furious 7 &amp; 8

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:09.140
If you're new to the Fast and the Furious
franchise, you might be surprised to learn

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.500
that a lot of the stunts you see on screen
are legit.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:16.560
Like that scene in Furious 7 where Vin Diesel
and the gang parachute from a plane 12,000

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.260
feet in the air — in their cars.

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:22.600
The filmmakers rigged up four vehicles with
parachutes and dropped them from the sky.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:26.440
Each car was equipped with three cameras,
and three skydivers with helmet cameras also

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:27.420
went along for the jump.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:32.080
In The Fate of the Furious, the gang zooms
into Berlin, and ends up smashing bad guys

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.840
into oblivion with the help of a 14.5-ton
wrecking ball.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:40.060
For the scene, the stunt drivers doubling
for the good guys had to get out of the way,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.820
but the other driver-less vehicles were towed
at 40 miles per hour, straight into the equally

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:46.280
fast ball of death.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:47.880
Baby Driver

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.100
Director Edgar Wright basically mashed up
Heat, The Driver, and Singin' in the Rain

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:53.780
into one glorious movie.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.970
This 2017 heist flick follows a music-loving
getaway driver who tries to leave his life

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:02.120
of crime after falling head-over-heels for
a charming waitress.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.980
And as it turns out, almost every stunt Baby
pulls off is the real deal.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:11.820
That includes the jaw-dropping "180 in, 180
out" gag from the movie's opening scene.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.740
Our hero throws his Subaru into a forward
180, spinning around one truck, before throwing

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:20.720
the car into a reverse 180, successfully dodging
another vehicle.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.980
The Baby Driver stunt doubles wanted to capture
that realistic feel, even if it meant putting

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:26.260
their lives on the line.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:29.080
"Questions?"

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.120
Thanks for watching!

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.180
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:35.420
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

